I'm assuming TypeScript/Node and a `Booking` shape like the one below, so adjust the fields to match your model. It has no dependencies.

```typescript
export interface Booking {
  id: string;
  title: string;
  start: Date;
  end: Date;
  status: "confirmed" | "pending" | "cancelled";
  updatedAt: Date;
  description?: string;
  location?: string;
  url?: string;
  revision?: number; // increment on each change, if you track it
}

export interface EventOptions {
  /** Used in UIDs, e.g. "example.com". Must stay constant forever. */
  uidDomain: string;
  /** "omit" removes cancelled bookings from the feed; "mark" emits STATUS:CANCELLED. */
  cancelled?: "omit" | "mark";
}

const CRLF = "\r\n";

/** Escape TEXT values per RFC 5545 §3.3.11. */
function escapeText(value: string): string {
  return value
    .replace(/\\/g, "\\\\")
    .replace(/;/g, "\\;")
    .replace(/,/g, "\\,")
    .replace(/\r\n|\r|\n/g, "\\n");
}

/** Format a Date as UTC iCal date-time: 20250131T093000Z */
function formatUtc(date: Date): string {
  return date.toISOString().replace(/[-:]/g, "").replace(/\.\d{3}/, "");
}

/** Fold a content line to max 75 octets without splitting multi-byte characters. */
function foldLine(line: string): string {
  const MAX = 75;
  const parts: string[] = [];
  let current = "";
  let bytes = 0;

  for (const ch of line) {
    const size = Buffer.byteLength(ch, "utf8");
    if (bytes + size > MAX) {
      parts.push(current);
      current = " "; // continuation lines start with one space (counts toward the limit)
      bytes = 1;
    }
    current += ch;
    bytes += size;
  }
  parts.push(current);
  return parts.join(CRLF);
}

function bookingToVEvent(booking: Booking, opts: EventOptions): string {
  if (!(booking.end > booking.start)) {
    throw new Error(`Booking ${booking.id}: end must be after start`);
  }

  const lines: (string | null)[] = [
    "BEGIN:VEVENT",
    `UID:booking-${booking.id}@${opts.uidDomain}`,
    `DTSTAMP:${formatUtc(booking.updatedAt)}`,
    `LAST-MODIFIED:${formatUtc(booking.updatedAt)}`,
    `SEQUENCE:${booking.revision ?? 0}`,
    `DTSTART:${formatUtc(booking.start)}`,
    `DTEND:${formatUtc(booking.end)}`,
    `SUMMARY:${escapeText(booking.title)}`,
    booking.description ? `DESCRIPTION:${escapeText(booking.description)}` : null,
    booking.location ? `LOCATION:${escapeText(booking.location)}` : null,
    booking.url ? `URL:${booking.url}` : null,
    `STATUS:${
      booking.status === "cancelled" ? "CANCELLED"
      : booking.status === "pending" ? "TENTATIVE"
      : "CONFIRMED"
    }`,
    "END:VEVENT",
  ];

  return lines
    .filter((l): l is string => l !== null)
    .map(foldLine)
    .join(CRLF);
}

/** Convert bookings into an array of VEVENT blocks (no trailing CRLF). */
export function bookingsToVEvents(bookings: Booking[], opts: EventOptions): string[] {
  const cancelled = opts.cancelled ?? "omit";
  return bookings
    .filter((b) => cancelled === "mark" || b.status !== "cancelled")
    .map((b) => bookingToVEvent(b, opts));
}

/** Thin wrapper to produce a complete .ics body, which you'll need for the feed anyway. */
export function buildCalendar(
  bookings: Booking[],
  opts: EventOptions & { prodId?: string; name?: string }
): string {
  const header = [
    "BEGIN:VCALENDAR",
    "VERSION:2.0",
    `PRODID:${opts.prodId ?? "-//Example Co//Bookings//EN"}`,
    "CALSCALE:GREGORIAN",
    "METHOD:PUBLISH",
    opts.name ? `X-WR-CALNAME:${escapeText(opts.name)}` : null,
  ].filter((l): l is string => l !== null).map(foldLine);

  return [...header, ...bookingsToVEvents(bookings, opts), "END:VCALENDAR"].join(CRLF) + CRLF;
}
```

Some of the choices are worth knowing about:

- **Stable UIDs:** `booking-<id>@domain` never changes for a given booking, so calendar apps update the existing event instead of duplicating it.
- **Deterministic DTSTAMP:** it uses `updatedAt` rather than `now()`, so the feed output doesn't change between fetches unless a booking changed. This also lets you serve ETag or `Last-Modified` headers.
- **UTC times:** this avoids needing `VTIMEZONE` blocks. Events show at the correct moment in each user's local time. If you later need recurring events that follow DST, or all-day bookings, you'll need to extend this.
- **Cancelled bookings:** the default is to omit them, which is the most reliable way to make them disappear from subscribed calendars on refresh. `"mark"` emits `STATUS:CANCELLED` instead, but Google Calendar's handling of that in subscribed feeds is inconsistent.
- **Escaping and folding:** commas, semicolons, backslashes, and newlines are escaped, and lines are folded at 75 octets with CRLF endings. Skipping these is the most common reason feeds work in one client and break in another.

When you build the feed endpoint, serve it as `text/calendar; charset=utf-8`. Also keep sensitive details out of `DESCRIPTION` and `SUMMARY` where you can, since the secret URL is the only protection and calendar apps may store or sync that data through third-party servers.

